Z||000000||jlhuber||05-15-2000||12:31 AM||||while 262 miles (somerset, PA) into my 760 mile trip to parents house from new jersey to michigan for a relatives wedding, the tranny in my '86gt let go.  i ended up having dad come out with his truck and car trailer to haul me and my gt the rest of the way.  the 7.3 turbo diesel in dad's '99 f-250 did an excellent job hauling the car.   anywho, enough rambling and on to my question.  i have always wanted a 5spd (6spd would be nice but pricey) in my automatic gt.  i have found a boneyard that will give me a 5spd with linkage, pedals, master cyl, everything to convert it to a manual for about $400-$500.  the 5spd tranny is said to be out of an '86 gt with 30,000 original miles on it.  my question(s) are, does this sound like a decent price, and about how long on average would it take to remove the auto tranny and install the manual?  thanx for any help you guys and gals can give to a fellow fieroite.  -Jess||||reg||8
Z||000001||DRH||05-15-2000||03:11 PM||||Haven't actually done this yet. I am currently in the thinking about it stage on my 84-put-a-good-engine-from-a-wreck-into-another-body-project. <P>Some things not to forget:<BR>- Axles (passenger side for sure is different)<BR>- ECM chip<BR>- Wiring: clutch pedal starter interlock switch, clutch pedal cruise control switch, trunk release/ ebrake interlock, backup lights?, other???<BR>- you will probably want to put in a new clutch while you have it out.<P>Price does not sound bad... especially if they will give you EVERYTHING you need to do the swap.<P>Figure how long you think it'll take... double that for things you forgot you'd have to do... double again for rusted bolts, etc... should be close to actual time then  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-15-2000||03:58 PM||||You can't move chips from one type of ECM to the other, so get the whole ECM. Even if they are the same ECM, a spare isn't a bad thing. ||||reg||1
